{"availability":"available","full":"bigomics/plaid","name":"plaid","description":"Ultrafast method to compute single-sample gene set enrichment scores for gene expression or proteomics data","category":"Other","language":"R","stars":24,"forks":3,"created":"2025-05-23T09:37:19Z","updated":"2026-09-25T23:21:11Z","license":"GPL-3.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://bigomics.github.io/plaid/","topics":["bioinformatics","enrichment-analysis","omics-data","rna-seq-analysis"],"demo":"https://bigomics.github.io/plaid/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T16:44:01.519Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T16:44:01.519Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T16:44:01.519Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T16:44:01.519Z","stars":24,"forks":3}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["PLAID (Pathway Level Average Intensity Detection) is an ultrafast method to compute single-sample enrichment scores for gene expression or proteomics data. For each sample, PLAID computes the gene set score as the average intensity of the genes/proteins in the gene set. The output is a gene set score matrix suitable for further analyses.","A distinctive feature of PLAID is that it can simulate few of the most widely used single-sample gene set scoring algorithms (ssGSEA, GSVA, AUCell, singscore, scSE, UCell), enabling researchers to replace those functions and gain much improved runtime efficiency and memory requirement. Typically, PLAID can be more than 100 times faster and requiring 10 times less memory than the original algorithm."],"features":["Ultra-fast single-sample gene set enrichment scoring","Includes multiple scoring methods (plaid, singscore, ssGSEA, GSVA, scSE, UCell, AUCell)","Works with regular matrices, sparse matrices, and Bioconductor data structures","Automatically detects and handles Bioconductor objects","(SummarizedExperiment, SingleCellExperiment, BiocSet)"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/bigomics/plaid/blob/main/README.md","fingerpr